0091_request_refusal_closed — NOT CARRIED AS A STEP, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS REAL.

The shared entry adds a CHECK to `organizer_requests.refused_reason`, closing it to the eight
members of REQUEST_REFUSAL_REASONS and NULL. This journal does not carry it, and the honest reason
is not "the write path is already closed" — the shared entry's own header rejects that argument, in
the same words, because an argument about the write path stops being true the day somebody adds a
second write path.

The reason is that the constraint cannot be carried on one side alone. The Postgres twin
(`schema-mail.ts`) does not DECLARE this CHECK — it exists in the migration and nowhere else — and
`schema-twin-parity.test.ts` compares the two twins' CHECK names. Declaring it here and not there
fails parity; declaring it in both is an edit to the shared server schema, which this journal is
not the place to make.

WHAT IS THEREFORE TRUE ON A DEVICE: an out-of-vocabulary refusal reason would be refused by the
server's database and stored by this one. Nothing writes such a value — the reason is parsed from a
payload verified under the account's key and anything outside the vocabulary is mapped to NULL
before it reaches SQL — so this is a difference in what the STORE would refuse, not in what the
program does.

NOT OWED ANY MORE, and the earlier advice here was the opposite of the rule. This file used to
say the constraint should be declared on both twins and added to `0001_baseline.sql`. Mail 0094
then widened it, which makes it a WIDENABLE set: SQLite cannot drop a CHECK, so carrying it here
would have frozen a table rebuild into the first shipped baseline for one more refusal reason.

A widenable set is refused at the write door instead — the payload's reason is parsed under the
account's key and anything outside the vocabulary is mapped to NULL before it reaches SQL, which
is that door. `closed-set-parity.test.ts` is what keeps the two halves honest.
